{
 "case": "BKP-PASSIVE",
 "says": "On her laptop Alice registers her private notes source, commits a revision on the head the registration made, and takes a backup under access generation 7. The laptop keeps running; the backup is restored onto a second runtime, a restored copy holding the same device's data and that old access generation. The restore reports authority passive. A commit on the restored copy, expecting the head the backup holds and carrying the restored generation 7, is blocked with access_locked and access_generation_moved and moves nothing: the restored copy did not unlock itself and is not a second finalizer. Reading the profile there shows it locked because restored, at generation 8, past the one it restored. On the laptop, meanwhile, a commit expecting that same head is accepted, so the laptop stays the one runtime that moves this head. Only an explicit unlock at a terminal on the restored copy, expecting generation 8, makes its access active, as a new access state the unlock wrote at that same generation; a query there then returns the restored commit's original result and receipt. What must not happen: the restored copy reading its restored generation as active access, unlocking without a person's explicit unlock, or finalizing a commit beside the laptop.",
